#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129145 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2f03be7e-0a77-5869-b591-6a8f8f3942de" :question/math-academy-id "e-5814" :question/problem "![](images/59/5970275bd70bcfd944c03abc8f60c2b159e3545dc876a0dd05bc23343b85bdec.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial above?\n\n- $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n\n- $y=(x+2)^3(x+1)^2(x-2)$\n\n- $y=(x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x =-2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x =-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x = 2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y= (x+2)(x+1)^2(x-2)^3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517437 :question-generator/id #uuid "4a7ba8fc-a33a-5fbc-aeb0-5a5bc71de1d6" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186129145.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83b06151-1ea9-5064-8aff-08bf63e0d416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/mat